<Base
  title="Andorra tax residence | Official Art. 8 IRPF note"
  description="Who is an Andorran tax resident under Article 8 of Law 5/2014: 183 days or the centre of economic interests. Not the same as a D.1 immigration permit."
  path="/tax-residence"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Tax residence in Andorra</h1>
    <p>
      An immigration permit is not, by itself, tax residence. The
      Government’s own FAQ quotes the income-tax law. We quote that FAQ —
      not a headline rate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Source:
        <a href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/l/4191384" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
          >“A l’efecte de ser obligat tributari de l’impost, qui es
          considera resident fiscal a Andorra?” — Govern d’Andorra</a
        >, citing article 8 of Law 5/2014 of 24 April on personal income
        tax. The taxes theme is
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/impostos-taxes-i-duana"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Impostos, taxes i duana</a
        >. We do not quote rates, bands or double-tax outcomes. If the
        official text changes, it wins.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Who the FAQ treats as a tax resident</h2>
    <p>
      Natural persons are treated as tax residents in Andorra — and
      therefore as taxpayers for the tax — if any of the following
      circumstances applies. That is the Government’s wording, not ours.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        They remain more than 183 days, during the calendar year, in
        Andorran territory. Sporadic absences count toward that period,
        unless the taxpayer proves tax residence in another country.
      </li>
      <li>
        The main core or base of their activities or economic interests
        is in Andorran territory, directly or indirectly.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      There is also a presumption, unless the contrary is proved: the
      taxpayer is treated as tax resident in Andorra when, under those
      same criteria, the spouse who is not legally separated and the
      minor children are tax residents in Andorra.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this is not your D.1 diary</h2>
    <p>
      Several residence-without-work pages — including
      <a href="/residence-without-work">D.1</a>
      and
      <a href="/digital-nomad">D.3</a>
      — speak of principal and effective residence for at least 90 days
      per calendar year. That is an immigration condition on those
      pages. Article 8 of the income-tax law uses 183 days, counts
      sporadic absences, and adds a centre-of-interests test. Spending
      ninety days in Andorra does not, on the face of those two official
      texts, answer the tax question.
    </p>
    <p>
      The country you leave may still treat you as its tax resident.
      Andorra publishes information-exchange material under the
      Ministeri de Finances. Do not treat a permit letter as a clearance
      from HMRC, the Agencia Tributaria or the DGFiP.
    </p>

    <h2>What we will not add</h2>
    <p>
      We will not invent a 10% rate, a lump-sum regime, or a “typical”
      first-year bill. Those belong on the live tax pages and on your
      advisor’s reading of them. Orientation:
      <a href="/become-a-resident">permit types</a>,
      <a href="/moving-to-andorra">moving to Andorra</a>,
      <a href="/official-sources">official sources</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

